WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:34.417
Measure the wait. The application is slow. Inspect whether requests wait on CPU, memory, storage, locks or another service before selecting a bigger machine.

2
00:00:34.417 --> 00:01:09.167
Increase resources. Give the same database more appropriate resources. This is vertical scaling because the number of independent database copies has not increased.

3
00:01:09.167 --> 00:01:46.083
Watch useful memory. More memory can hold more of the hot working set. If disk reads were the bottleneck, the experiment may show lower latency.

4
00:01:46.083 --> 00:02:22.333
Plan interruption. A size change may restart or fail over the database. Use the actual platform procedure and verify what users see during the transition.

5
00:02:22.333 --> 00:02:54.750
Retest the result. Repeat the same controlled workload. Compare throughput, tail latency, waits and cost; do not use machine size as the success metric.

6
00:02:54.750 --> 00:03:34.417
Keep a recovery plan. The larger machine still needs redundancy and backups. If the machine fails or reaches its size ceiling, a different design decision is needed.
